    "['term [term-number] from route-filter [route-filter] exact', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'route-map ROUTE-MAP-1 permit 10', 'parent_command': ['interface Tunnel0'], 'root': 'interface Tunnel0', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Cisco equivalent command for the given Juniper configuration:\\n\\n##\\nroute-map ROUTE-MAP-1 permit 10\\n match ip address prefix-list 192.168.0.0/24\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- Juniper's `policy-statement` translates to Cisco's `route-map`\\n- `term 1` becomes a sequence number (10 is commonly used as the first sequence)\\n- `from route-filter` becomes `match ip address prefix-list` in Cisco\\n- The `/24 exact` match is implied by using a prefix-list with the exact network in Cisco\\n- The `then accept` is represented by the `permit` keyword in the route-map\\n\\nNote: You would also need a corresponding prefix-list configuration in Cisco, like:\\n```\\nip prefix-list 192.168.0.0/24 seq 10 permit 192.168.0.0/24\\n```\\nto make this route-map fully functional.\", 'trans_template': 'route-map ROUTE-MAP-1 permit 10'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'match ip address prefix-list 192.168.0.0/24', 'parent_command': ['interface Tunnel0'], 'root': 'interface Tunnel0', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Cisco equivalent command for the given Juniper configuration:\\n\\n##\\nroute-map ROUTE-MAP-1 permit 10\\n match ip address prefix-list 192.168.0.0/24\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- Juniper's `policy-statement` translates to Cisco's `route-map`\\n- `term 1` becomes a sequence number (10 is commonly used as the first sequence)\\n- `from route-filter` becomes `match ip address prefix-list` in Cisco\\n- The `/24 exact` match is implied by using a prefix-list with the exact network in Cisco\\n- The `then accept` is represented by the `permit` keyword in the route-map\\n\\nNote: You would also need a corresponding prefix-list configuration in Cisco, like:\\n```\\nip prefix-list 192.168.0.0/24 seq 10 permit 192.168.0.0/24\\n```\\nto make this route-map fully functional.\", 'trans_template': 'match ip address prefix-list 192.168.0.0/24'}]]": 1,
    "['set policy-options policy-statement [policy-statement]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'interface GigabitEthernet1/0/0', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'interface GigabitEthernet1/0/0', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translation of the Juniper command to Cisco IOS:\\n\\nFor the Juniper command:\\n```\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 tunnel encapsulation gre\\n```\\n\\nThe equivalent Cisco command would be:\\n##\\ninterface GigabitEthernet1/0/0\\n tunnel mode gre ip\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Cisco IOS, GRE tunneling is configured directly on the physical interface rather than a logical unit, and the tunnel mode command specifies GRE encapsulation. The Cisco command assumes you're already in interface configuration mode.\", 'trans_template': 'interface GigabitEthernet1/0/0'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'tunnel mode gre ip', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'tunnel mode gre ip', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translation of the Juniper command to Cisco IOS:\\n\\nFor the Juniper command:\\n```\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 tunnel encapsulation gre\\n```\\n\\nThe equivalent Cisco command would be:\\n##\\ninterface GigabitEthernet1/0/0\\n tunnel mode gre ip\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Cisco IOS, GRE tunneling is configured directly on the physical interface rather than a logical unit, and the tunnel mode command specifies GRE encapsulation. The Cisco command assumes you're already in interface configuration mode.\", 'trans_template': 'tunnel mode gre ip'}]]": 1,
